course: dictionary
lesson: 8636
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and scuttle is one of them. The context tells you which meaning the author means. «Payah kun,» says the Magician, laughing, and the tiny crab scuttles away. A crab is small and moves with quick, short steps, so scuttles means runs quickly with short steps.
explain: Now try a different sentence. «The rain scuttled our picnic.» Rain cannot run on tiny legs, so this scuttled means spoiled or stopped. Always test your meaning: put it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Scuttle can also be a noun, a thing rather than an action. In «grinning like a coal-scuttle», a coal scuttle is a metal bucket for coal. If the words around scuttle describe an action, it is probably a verb. If they describe a thing, it is probably a noun.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning, so the context decides which meaning is right.
fact: «Scuttle» means to run with short, quick steps, like a crab or a mouse.
fact: «Scuttle» can also mean to sink your own ship on purpose by making holes in it, as sailors once did so the enemy could not take it.
fact: «Scuttle» can mean to spoil or stop a plan, as in «the rain scuttled our picnic».
fact: A «scuttle» can be a noun: a metal bucket for coal.
exercise: In «the tiny crab scuttles away», what does scuttles mean? | To tidy up a room | To sing loudly | To sink a ship on purpose | *To run with short, quick steps
exercise: «The rain scuttled our picnic.» What does scuttled mean here? | To cook the food for the picnic | To wash the picnic plates | *To spoiled or stopped our plan | To hurry to the park
exercise: In «grinning like a coal-scuttle», what is a coal scuttle? | *A metal bucket for coal | A small black animal | A type of ship | A shovel for snow
test: Sailors scuttled their ship so the enemy could not take it. What does scuttled mean? | Sailed it very fast | *Sank it on purpose by making holes in it | Gave it to a friend | Painted it bright blue
test: «The mice scuttled into the hole.» Which meaning fits best? | To sink a ship on purpose | A metal bucket for coal | *To run with short, quick steps | To spoil or stop a plan
exam: Which two meanings fit the two uses of scuttle in «The storm scuttled our plans, so we scuttled indoors»? | *Spoil a plan, then run with short, quick steps | Wash the plates, then sleep | Pile up coal, then sing | Sink a ship, then sit down
